Executive Summary: Mathematical Foundations of an Energy-Aligned Financial Framework

Linking Arcseconds to ΔT (Delta T)

Earth’s rotation and orbit define our fundamental measures of time and energy flow. Critical to this is the subdivision of a full rotation into arcseconds:

1 full rotation = 360 degrees

1 degree = 60 arcminutes

1 arcminute = 60 arcseconds

Total arcseconds per rotation = 360 × 60 × 60 = 1,296,000


However, Earth’s solar year (~365.2425 days) slightly diverges from a perfect 360-degree model, resulting in a correction known as ΔT (Delta T), the difference between Terrestrial Dynamical Time (TD) and Universal Time (UT).

ΔT fluctuates over time due to tidal friction and other factors, correlating with shifts in arcsecond-based measurements. By mapping ΔT to arcseconds, our framework corrects timing drifts between Earth’s rotation and the solar calendar, enabling:

Precise synchronization of energy cycles with natural planetary rhythms

Accurate timekeeping essential for financial transactions and carbon accounting aligned with real-world energy flows



